    "case_summary": "A 9-year-old previously healthy girl presented with a several-week history of a progressively enlarging swelling on the posterior ulnar aspect of her right forearm. There was no history of trauma, systemic symptoms, or family history of similar lesions. On examination, a firm, non–tender 4 × 5 cm mass was palpated in the subcutaneous tissues of the distal forearm without overlying skin changes. Neurovascular examination of the hand was normal. \n\nPlain radiographs of the forearm showed no fracture but demonstrated soft-tissue swelling with cortical irregularity of the adjacent ulna. MRI of the right forearm revealed a mass arising from the bone and extending into the soft tissues. The lesion was isointense to muscle on T1-weighted images and heterogeneous hyperintense on T2-weighted images, with marked enhancement after gadolinium administration. The mass caused cortical bone lysis with medullary involvement.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Myofibroma\nB. nerve sheath tumors\nC. intramuscular myxoma\nD. intramuscular lipoma\nE. synovial sarcoma\nF. malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor\nG. muscle metastasis\nH. primary muscle tumors or other soft tissue sarcomas\n",
